12_23_23_28_17	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|NBF0|BBF0|QBF0|KBF0|BBF0|NBF0|RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': 'Okay, let\'s see. I\'m playing as White in a standard chess position. The board is set up with all the pieces in their starting positions. My valid moves are all the possible first moves for White. Since it\'s the beginning of the game, all pawns are on their second rank (for White) and haven\'t moved yet, so their Moved flag is 0 and 2Step is F.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the standard opening principles. Usually, in the opening, you want to control the center, develop your pieces, and ensure king safety. Common first moves for White are moving the e-pawn or d-pawn to e4, d4, e3, d3, etc. Knights to c3 or f3 are also common.\n\nLooking at the valid moves provided: the pawns can move one or two squares forward, and the knights on b1 and g1 can move to a3, c3, f3, h3.\n\nLet me list the possible good moves:\n\n1. e2-e4: This is the most popular opening move. It controls the center (d5 and f5 squares), opens lines for the queen and bishop. It\'s aggressive and leads to open games.\n\n2. d2-d4: Another strong central move, controlling e5 and c5. Leads to more closed or semi-closed positions.\n\n3. N: g1-f3: Developing the knight to f3, which supports e5 and prepares kingside castling. It\'s a solid developing move.\n\n4. e2-e3: A quieter move, preparing to support d4 later, but less aggressive than e4.\n\n5. d2-d3: Similar to e3, but less common as a first move.\n\nAmong these, e4 and d4 are the most common and considered strongest. Nf3 is also good but maybe slightly less aggressive for the first move.\n\nNow, considering the position: all pieces are in starting position. Black has all their pieces set up normally. As White, moving e4 is classic. It immediately stakes a claim in the center and allows the bishop and queen to develop.\n\nBut let\'s check if there\'s any reason not to play e4. Well, in some openings like the Scandinavian, Black can respond with d5, but that\'s manageable. Generally, e4 is a great move.\n\nOther pawn moves like c4 (but that\'s not a valid first move here; c2-c4 is listed) but c4 is the English opening, which is also good but maybe not as direct for center control as e4 or d4.\n\nWait, the valid moves include c2-c4. So c4 is an option. But as a first move, c4 is less common than e4 or d4. It\'s a flank opening.\n\nSimilarly, moving the knight to c3 (N: b1-c3) is possible, but usually, you move a pawn first to control the center before developing knights.\n\nSo between e4, d4, Nf3, which is best?\n\nIn terms of maximizing winning chances, e4 is probably the best. It\'s the most aggressive and leads to open games where White can play for a win.\n\nWait, the valid moves list shows P: e2-e4 and P: e2-e3. So e4 is an option.\n\nLet me check the valid moves again. The list is:\n\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\n\nSo e2-e4 is there. Similarly, d2-d4.\n\nIn top-level play, e4 and d4 are the main moves. e4 is slightly more popular historically.\n\nBut why not Nf3? Nf3 is a good move, but it doesn\'t control the center as directly as e4 or d4. However, it\'s a common move in some openings like the Reti. But as the very first move, e4 is stronger for immediate center control.\n\nAnother thing to consider: moving a pawn two squares (like e4) opens more lines for development. Moving one square (e3) is more conservative but less dynamic.\n\nGiven that the goal is to maximize winning chances, e4 is the most aggressive and common choice. It\'s the move that leads to the most tactical and complex positions, which is good for trying to win.\n\nAre there any downsides? Well, if Black plays the Sicilian (c5), it can lead to sharp lines, but that\'s manageable. Alternatively, if Black plays e5, it\'s the Open Game.\n\nBut as the first move, e4 is solid.\n\nSo between e4, d4, Nf3. Let\'s compare:\n\n- e4: controls d5 and f5, opens diagonal for bishop and queen.\n\n- d4: controls e5 and c5, opens for queen and bishop.\n\n- Nf3: develops knight, prepares castling, but doesn\'t control the center as much immediately.\n\nIn terms of center control, e4 and d4 are better. Between e4 and d4, e4 is more common and leads to more open positions.\n\nTherefore, the best move here is likely e2-e4.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any specific reason to prefer another move. For example, in some cases, d4 might be better if you want to avoid certain lines, but generally, e4 is the go-to.\n\nAnother angle: the valid moves include P: e2-e4, which is a standard move. The answer should be exactly as per the valid list. So the move is "P: e2-e4".\n\nYes, that\'s in the list. So the answer should be P: e2-e4.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if any other move might be better. For example, in some situations, moving the knight first could be better, but in the initial position, pawn moves to center are preferred.\n\nAlso, moving a pawn to the fourth rank (like a4) is generally not good as it doesn\'t control the center and wastes time.\n\nSo yes, e2-e4 is the strongest move here.', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the starting position with all pieces on their initial squares. As White, I have the first-move advantage and a full set of valid opening moves, primarily pawn advances to the third or fourth rank and knight developments. The board is symmetric, and no immediate threats exist—this is a pure strategic decision to establish early control.\n\nEvaluation: Strong opening principles prioritize central control (e4/d4), piece development, and king safety. Key options include:\n- **P: e2-e4**: Maximizes central control (dominating d5/f5), opens diagonals for the queen and light-squared bishop, and leads to dynamic, tactical positions (e.g., Open Games). Statistically, this is the most winning-oriented first move in practice.\n- **P: d2-d4**: Solid central claim (e5/c5), but slightly less aggressive than e4 and often leads to more positional play.\n- **N: g1-f3**: Develops a knight and prepares kingside castling, but delays central pawn commitment and cedes immediate center initiative.\n- **P: e2-e3/d2-d3**: Conservative, but wastes tempo by not challenging the center directly; better suited for specific setups later.\n\nReason: **P: e2-e4** best aligns with aggressive opening theory. It immediately stakes the strongest claim in the center, accelerates development, and creates winning chances by inviting sharp play (e.g., Sicilian Defense or Open Games). While d4 is viable, e4 historically yields higher win rates for White at all levels. Knight moves or quiet pawn pushes (e3/d3) are less optimal here as they surrender the initiative.\n\nAnswer: P: e2-e4'}
