ow did it get to d5? Let\'s see. The black pawns on 7th rank, so normally they move down. Wait, no: in chess, pawns move forward, which for Black is down the board (from rank 8 to 1). Wait, but in the board representation, White is at the bottom (ranks 1-2), Black at top (7-8). So Black pawns move from rank 7 to 8? No, wait, standard chess: White pawns move up (from 2 to 7), Black pawns move down (from 7 to 2). Wait, but in the problem statement: "White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)", so White\'s pawns start on rank 2, moving towards rank 8. Black\'s pawns start on rank 7, moving towards rank 1. Wait, no: in standard chess, White\'s pawns start on 2nd rank, moving up to 8th. Black\'s start on 7th, moving down to 1st. So in this board, White is at bottom (ranks 1-2), so their pawns are on rank 2, moving up. Black\'s pawns are on rank 7, moving down.\n\nSo the black pawn at d5 (rank 5) must have moved from d7 to d5, which is two squares, hence PBT1 (2Step T). Similarly, the white pawn at e4 (rank 4) is PWT1, so it moved from e2 to e4, two squares, hence en passant possible.\n\nNow, the valid moves for White include some en passant possibilities. Let\'s check the valid moves list.\n\nLooking at the valid moves provided:\n\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, K: e1-e2, P: e4-d5, P: e4-e5, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-e2, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h3, P: h2-h4]\n\nWait, the move P: e4-d5 is listed. That\'s a pawn capture. Because e4 is white pawn, and d5 is black pawn. But since the black pawn moved two squares to d5, en passant is possible. So if white pawn on e4 captures the black pawn on d5 as if it had only moved one square, that\'s en passant. So e4xd5 would be the en passant capture.\n\nWait, but in the valid moves, it\'s listed as P: e4-d5. So that\'s the en passant move.\n\nSimilarly, the black pawn