wns, when they move two squares, the 2Step becomes T. For other pieces, maybe 2Step is irrelevant? Because only pawns can move two squares. So for non-pawns, 2Step is probably always F.\n\nWait, the problem statement says: "2Step (En Passant flag): F - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn; T - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)". So only pawns can have 2Step T, because other pieces don\'t move two squares in a way that would trigger en passant. So for non-pawn pieces, 2Step is always F. So in the board state, for example, RBF0 is Black Rook, hasn\'t moved (Moved 0), and 2Step F. Similarly, BBF1 is Black Bishop that has moved (Moved 1), 2Step F.\n\nSo in rank 6, d6 has BBF1, which is a Black Bishop that has moved before.\n\nRank 5:\na5: PBF1 (Black pawn that has moved, and 2Step F? Wait, PBF1: Type P (Pawn), Color B (Black), 2Step F, Moved 1. So this pawn has moved before, but didn\'t move two squares last turn. Then d5: PWF1 (White pawn, moved, 2Step F? Wait, PWF1: P (Pawn), W (White), 2Step F, Moved 1. So this is a White pawn on d5 that has moved, but didn\'t move two squares last turn. Wait, but how did it get to d5? Maybe it was pushed from d2 to d4 (two squares), but then 2Step would be T. Wait, but here it\'s PWF1, which is 2Step F. Hmm. Maybe the encoding for pawns: when a pawn moves two squares, the 2Step becomes T, and Moved becomes 1. So for example, a pawn moving from d2 to d4 would be PWF T (but the encoding is [2Step][Moved], so maybe the third character is 2Step (T/F), fourth is Moved (1/0). Wait, the problem statement says:\n\nPiece encoding format: [Type][Color][2Step][Moved]\n\nSo for a pawn that just moved two squares (en passant eligible), it\'s P W T 1? Because it has moved (so Moved=1), and 2Step=T.\n\nWait, looking at the example given: PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant (so 2Step=F), already moved (Moved=1). So the third character is 2Step (F/T), fourth is Moved (0/1). So PWF1 is Pawn, White, 2Step=F, Moved=1.\n\nSimilarly,