oking at the valid moves, there\'s no knight moves from b1, so likely that knight is gone (captured). So currently, White has only one knight on g1.\n\nSimilarly, Black\'s knights: on b8 and g8 (NBF0), so both present.\n\nNow, looking at the position, White\'s pawns on rank 2: a2, b2, c2, d2, f2, g2, h2 (e2 is empty). So e2 is empty, which is probably where the king\'s bishop was developed or something.\n\nWait, the valid moves include P: d2-d4, which would be pushing the d-pawn two squares. But d2 is a White pawn (PWF0: hasn\'t moved). Wait, the encoding for d2 is PWF0: Pawn, White, 2Step=F (since it hasn\'t moved yet, so 2Step is irrelevant, but according to the example, PWF0 would be a White pawn that hasn\'t moved, so 2Step=F and Moved=0. Wait, the example says: "PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);"\n\nSo PWF0 would be Pawn White, 2Step=F (since it hasn\'t moved, so couldn\'t have moved two squares), Moved=0 (hasn\'t moved yet). So the pawns on rank 2 (a2, b2, etc.) are all PWF0, meaning they haven\'t moved yet.\n\nWait, but in the board state for rank 2:\n\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|    |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n\nSo all pawns on rank 2 are PWF0 (haven\'t moved). Except e2 is empty.\n\nNow, looking at Black\'s position. On rank 7, Black has pawns on b7, c7, f7, g7, h7 (a7 and d7, e7 are empty). On rank 5, a5 has a Black pawn (PBF1), d5 has a White pawn (PWF1), e5 has a Black pawn (PBF1). Wait, rank 5:\n\n5 |PBF1|    |    |PWF1|PBF1|    |    |    | 5\n\nSo a5: Black pawn (PBF1), d5: White pawn (PWF1), e5: Black pawn (PBF1).\n\nSo there\'s a White pawn on d5, which is advanced. How did it get there? From d2 to d3 to d4 to d5, perhaps. So it\'s on d5, and since it\'s PWF1 (Moved=1), it\'s moved before.\n\nAlso, on rank 6, d6 has a Black bishop (BBF1).\n\nNow, looking for threats. Is the White king in check? Let\'s see. The Black pieces: on e8 is the Black king. Black\'s bishops: c8 and d6. Black\'